These agents can search, evaluate, and eventually transact on the consumer's behalf. The
article's core diagnosis is precise: we have agentic participants, but not yet agentic markets
— because intelligence is accumulating on only one side of the market.
This paper accepts that diagnosis but asks the question the article does not: on what
architecture can such a consumer agent run without inheriting the four walls of current LLM
agents?
The four walls are measurable: energy (global AI data centre power capacity has reached
29.6 GW, equivalent to New York State's peak demand); water (direct data centre cooling
water could rise from 222 billion litres in 2025 to 644 billion litres by 2030); hallucination
(22% to 94% across 26 models tested); and liability (Amazon blocked Meta's Muse on
September 21, 2026, citing unauthorized agent access).
This paper argues that ST-T1024's agentic extension A0–A10 provides the deterministic
foundation for a consumer agent, and A11–A16 provides the cross-organizational layer.
Together, they present a consumer agent that is architecturally incapable of producing the
four walls — not by making the LLM safer, but by removing the LLM from the decision path.
---
1. The Agentic Market, and Its Gap
1.1 The Moment the Article Captures
The article "Agents and their better half" describes a real structural shift in the agent
economy. Muse and Instinct are "a market-facing representative of the consumer" that
"knows enough about an individual to act on that individual's behalf." The article's central
distinction is:
· Agentic participants: one-sided intelligence that improves navigation of an existing market.
The buyer becomes adaptive; supply remains pre-composed.
· Agentic markets: intelligence on both sides, where agents "mutually construct an
executable offer" rather than one side selecting from a catalogue.
The author's diagnosis is direct and correct: "We have agentic participants but don't have
agentic markets yet."
1.2 The Question the Article Does Not Answer
The article describes what should happen — agents on both sides negotiating, mutually
constructing offers. It does not describe what architecture this happens on.
The implicit assumption is that these will be LLM agents, negotiating through natural
language or tool-calling protocols. But the article's own examples already show the cost of
that assumption: Indeed paused Apply For Me after employer feedback; Amazon blocked
Muse for "unauthorized agent access."
These are not bugs to be patched. They are consequences of building market infrastructure
on a stochastic substrate.
1.3 The Four Walls
The four walls are not rhetoric. They are measurements.
Energy. AI data centre power capacity reached 29.6 GW by the end of 2025 — equivalent to
New York State's peak demand, comparable to the entire electricity consumption of
Switzerland or Austria. Data centre power demand has already reached roughly 47,000 MW.
Water. Data centres consumed 222 billion litres of water for direct cooling in 2025. Rystad
Energy estimates this could nearly triple to 644 billion litres by 2030 absent water-saving
measures. GPT-4o alone may consume enough water annually for over 1.2 million people's
drinking needs.
Hallucination. Across 26 models tested, hallucination rates range from 22% to 94%. Grok
4.20 Beta was lowest at 22%; GPT-OSS-20B highest at 94%. In an agent-based negotiation
study, agents made false claims in 88% of sessions.
Liability. Amazon blocked Muse on September 21, 2026. Amazon's statement: "Continued
access by an unauthorized AI agent violates Amazon's Conditions of Use." The specific
objections: Muse failed to identify itself when browsing, captured customer credentials, and
raised privacy and security concerns. When an LLM agent hallucinates a transaction, who
bears the cost — this question has no answer in the current stack.
1.4 The Four Walls Are One Failure
Energy, water, hallucination, and liability are four faces of one architectural failure: placing a
stochastic, open-ended generator in the decision path of market transactions.
· Energy/Water: Stochastic inference scales with prompt volume, not with verified outcomes.
· Hallucination: The loss function rewards fluency, not truth. No mechanism checks output
against ground truth before emission.
· Liability: An LLM agent cannot be held accountable for a "decision" it did not
deterministically make. The trace does not exist.
ST-T1024's answer is architectural: remove the LLM from the decision path.
---
2. Recall: ST-T1024 and the Agentic Extension
2.1 Core Commitments
ST-T1024 is a deterministic inference architecture for embedded LLMs. Its defining
commitments:
· Determinism by construction: Temperature fixed at 0.0. Same input, same output,
bit-identical. Simulation equals benchmarking.
· FIS triage: Inputs routed to exactly one domain tile by a ROM-burned fuzzy inference
system.
· FSM scheduling: Execution under a bounded cycle counter with a declared Assured
Execution Time (AET).
· Ternary logic: Values in {−1, 0, +1}. XNOR + popcount arithmetic. No floating-point
multipliers in the hot path.
· Tile independence: Tiles share no knowledge by reference. Shared facts are duplicated in
each tile, independently signed and attested.
· Refusal as first-class output: 0x-AMBIG is not an error condition. It is the safety state.
The core claim: the system's behavior is deterministic, finite, and enumerable — regardless
of the underlying model's internal character.
2.2 The Agentic Extension A0–A10
The agentic extension moves ST-T1024 from a deterministic oracle to a certified actor. Its
central theorem:
Verifiable Agency Theorem. Given: (i) a finite action library A, ROM-burned; (ii) a finite plan
library P, ROM-burned; (iii) an FSM with bounded transition function and bounded T-AET;
and (iv) formal preconditions and postconditions for every action — the complete behavior
set B = {all possible action sequences the agent can produce} is finite, enumerable, and
exhaustively verifiable before deployment.
Mechanisms: A1 (Extended FSM), A2 (ROM-burned Action Library), A5 (VERIFY gate), A6
(Tile Invocation Protocol), A7 (Persistent Deterministic Memory), A8 (Goal Parsing and Plan
Library), A9 (Bounded Task Execution), A10 (Verifiable Agency Theorem).
Consequence: the system cannot act outside B. This is the property that makes certification
possible.
2.3 The Consumer Agent Concrete Case
The article describes a consumer agent: a Muse or Instinct that searches, evaluates, and
eventually transacts.
In ST-T1024, that agent is instantiated as:
· State: consumer intent declared as a finite set of fields (goal class, price constraints,
merchant allowlist, deadline, spend cap).
· Actions: ROM-burned action library — search, filter, compare, add-to-cart, initiate
transaction, refuse, escalate-to-consumer.
· Plans: plan library — PLAN_BROWSE, PLAN_COMPARE, PLAN_TRANSACT,
PLAN_HANDOFF.
· Trajectory predicates: sequence-level checks over a window W — spend cap, authorized
action classes, merchant allowlist.
· EAR (Environment Assumption Register): declared bounds on price accuracy, inventory
availability, merchant response validity. Bound violation → refuse.
The agent selects actions. It does not generate them. If an action is not in B, or is blocked by
a trajectory predicate, or violates the EAR, the result is 0x-AMBIG — not a hallucination, not
a confident guess, but a refusal.
2.4 The Cross-Organizational Extension A11–A16
The consumer agent does not exist alone. It meets platform agents, merchant agents,
payment processors. A11–A16 provides the language for that meeting:
· A11 — Agent Certificate: signed, versioned artifact containing B, P, hazard matrix, EAR,
refusal floor ε, ROM hash, signature chain.
· A12 — Intent Envelope: consumer-signed, scoped, revocable delegation token. Every
action traceable to an envelope clause.
· A13 — Counterparty Admission Protocol: platform publishes its own machine-readable
contract — accepted actions, error bounds, refusal semantics.
· A14 — Action Vocabulary Mapping: provable f: B_agent → B_platform ∪ {REFUSE}.
· A15 — Refusal as Wire Protocol: standardized refusal codes (0x-AMBIG-INTENT, -GOAL,
-ACTION, -STATE, -TRACE, -EPISTEMIC, -BUDGET).
· A16 — Cross-Organizational Trace Ledger: append-only, hash-chained, Merkle-anchored,
held by both parties.
These convert the conflict from an unresolvable commercial dispute into a decidable
engineering problem.
