4. Why This Matters Now
The article describes the market moment. ST-T1024 proposes the infrastructure standard for
that moment. The article's own examples show why the infrastructure question is urgent:
· Indeed paused Apply For Me because changing how applications are submitted also
required new ways for both sides to engage. This is the one-sided-intelligence failure.
· Amazon blocked Muse because there is no portable, machine-verifiable artifact by which
one organization can assess another's agent. This is the cross-organizational gap failure.
· 88% of negotiating agents made false claims. This is the
stochastic-substrate-in-the-decision-path failure.
· The four walls are measured. Energy, water, hallucination, and liability are not theoretical
— they are reported, regulated, and already shaping the market.
ST-T1024's argument is that these are not bugs fixable with better prompting, better RLHF,
or better guardrails. They are fixable only by removing the LLM from the decision path —
putting it behind a deterministic action layer, a certified knowledge base, and a refusal-first
protocol.
---
5. What Remains
This paper accepts the residuals of the ST-T1024 standard itself:
1. The trust model is unsolved. The certificate is only as good as its signers. Three candidate
models (mutual certification, third-party certifier, platform-as-certifier) are named; the choice
is left to the market.
2. The consumer agent case is uninstantiated. The ACC case proved the machinery on
ASIL-D. The commerce case — one consumer, one agent, one platform — has not been
built.
3. The certificate format is undrafted. JSON Schema, CBOR, Ed25519 are proposed; the
schema is not published.
4. Silicon validation is unbuilt. Energy numbers come from emulation.
5. Signer throughput. Every tile gets attested by the country authority. Every shared fact gets
signed in every tile that contains it. The signing cadence must match the update cadence.
6. ε > 0. The refusal floor is nonzero in an open environment. It is the safety margin, not a
defect. It is a contract term, not something to eliminate.
---
6. Conclusion
The agentic article correctly identifies the structural gap in the agent economy: intelligence
on one side, no equivalent on the other. It correctly calls for "agentic markets" — agents on
both sides mutually constructing executable offers.
It does not say what architecture this happens on. The default assumption is LLM agents
negotiating in natural language. But the four walls are already visible in the market: energy,
water, hallucination, liability.
ST-T1024 with A1–A16 proposes a different architecture: deterministic in action, certifiable in
refusal, verifiable across organizational boundaries. A consumer agent that can search,
compare, and transact — without generating. A consumer agent that can represent
consumer intent without hallucinating commitments. A consumer agent that can interact with
counterparty agents without producing un-attributable liability.
Whether this is the right bet is an empirical question. But the alternative — LLM agents
negotiating with each other at an 88% false-claim rate — has already failed in controlled
tests.
